@page "/admin"
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Http
@inject IAuthorizationService Authorization

<h1>Demo admin</h1>

<p class="lede">
    A console scoped to <em>your</em> demo session. It shows your orders and lets you move prices —
    and everything it writes belongs to you alone, which is the part worth looking at.
</p>

@if (SignedIn)
{
    <div class="banner">
        <strong>Signed in.</strong> This admin cookie was issued for the demo session you are already
        carrying, and it is checked against that session on every request. Copied into another
        browser it is inert.
    </div>

    <p><a href="/admin/orders">Your orders</a> · <a href="/admin/catalog">Your prices</a></p>

    <form class="inline" method="post" action="/api/admin/sign-out">
        <AntiforgeryToken />
        <button type="submit">Sign out</button>
    </form>
}
else
{
    <form method="post" action="/api/admin/sign-in">
        <AntiforgeryToken />
        <button class="primary" type="submit">Sign in as demo admin</button>
    </form>

    <p class="note">
        No password, because there is no account. The button issues a cookie bound to your own demo
        session — it takes no input, so there is no field naming a session and nothing to tamper
        with. A sign-in that accepted a session id would be an impersonation endpoint with a
        friendly name.
    </p>
}

<h2>What this admin deliberately cannot do</h2>

<p class="note">
    It cannot change the shop. Prices you set here land in a per-session overlay and the seeded
    catalog rows are never written — asserted in CI against PostgreSQL's own row-version column
    across products, variants and stock. It cannot adjust stock, because stock is the one ledger
    shared with every other visitor. And it cannot mark an order shipped, because shipping is what
    moves that ledger.
</p>

<p class="note">
    The cookie gates this console, not the data. Every query behind it runs through the same tenancy
    filter the shop uses, so removing the credential would lose the front door and change nothing
    about what a caller can reach.
</p>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Static SSR cascades the request's HttpContext to every component it renders, which is why
    /// this page takes no IHttpContextAccessor. That accessor is an ambient dependency — it works
    /// by reaching for whatever request happens to be in flight — and it is also not registered by
    /// default, so asking for it is a 500 on the first page view rather than a compile error.
    /// </summary>
    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext? HttpContext { get; set; }

    private bool SignedIn { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Evaluates the policy rather than reading IsAuthenticated, which is the distinction the
    /// banner above is about.
    /// <para>
    /// This is the only admin page without an [Authorize] attribute, because it is where a visitor
    /// signs in and therefore has to render for someone who has not. That made IsAuthenticated the
    /// obvious test, and it is the wrong one: authentication asks "is this ticket genuine", and a
    /// ticket lifted out of another browser is genuine. Only the policy asks the question the page
    /// claims to be asking — is this ticket genuine AND issued for the session in front of me —
    /// because that is where BoundToTheCallersSessionHandler runs. Until this evaluated it, /admin
    /// greeted a stolen cookie with "Signed in." while every guarded page correctly refused it.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// The other admin pages need nothing like this: [Authorize] means the framework has already
    /// run the same policy before the component is constructed.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    protected override async Task OnInitializedAsync()
    {
        SignedIn = HttpContext is not null
            && (await Authorization.AuthorizeAsync(HttpContext.User, DemoAdminAuthentication.Policy)).Succeeded;
    }
}
